+# tally FAQ
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+With pipx and Python 3.11 or newer: `pipx install tally-toolkit`. Check it with `tallyctl version`; if the command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release; the old name works as an alias for one more release, then goes away.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+8731. The September release closed the old port 8080, so anything still pointing at it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+`tallyctl cache clear` clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear one. The old purge command was removed and now fails as an unknown subcommand.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement (the earlier 30-minute rule is dropped). The order is primary on-call, then secondary, then the engineering manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1: customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 (a slow dashboard, a delayed batch) waits for the morning. Pages arrive in the Beacon app; install it and log in with SSO before your first shift.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Restart with a drain first: `tallyctl restart ingest --drain`. It finishes in-flight work, parks the stuck batch in the dead-letter table and starts the consumer clean; a plain restart does not help. Look for the cause afterwards.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew it, then restart the API, because only a restart serves the new certificate: `tallyctl certs renew api`, then `tallyctl restart api`. `tallyctl certs list` shows expiry dates.
